In article <3qalam$i...@darkstar.UCSC.EDU>,

Thomas R Wylie <aa...@cats.ucsc.edu> wrote:
}

} Masato Hayakawa <mas...@uclink.berkeley.edu> wrote:
} >To sum up, I guess I'm saying that the players have as much claim to
} >Magic as WotC/DC. Our individual visions of ways to play the game are
} >just as legitimate as Richard Garfield's (win-loss records aside ;-)).
} >The beauty of Magic is that both WotC's vision and our own can be
} >accomodated by the game--WotC should recognize that, rather than trying
} >to change the game to suit its own vision.
}
} Great. So follow your own vision, and play with all of the cards that
} you have. Heck, go wild and play with as many copies of those cards as you
} have. We really don't care. The goal here is to have fun, not to follow
} a restrictive set of tournament rules each and every time you play the game.
}
} We set up the tournament rules for tournaments, not for private play. Yes,
} I know people habitually build their decks to tournament rules, there's no
} reason you *have* to do that unless you're playing in a tournament, or testing
} out a tournament deck. And yes, the tournament rules make for a nice standard
} deck construction set when you're travelling and playing against new people.

I cannot believe that WotC is acting so dense about this particular
issue. It is really very simple: the rules of the game, as laid
out in the starter-deck rulebooks, do not work. They lead to
degenerate decks and boring games. So people *have to* find an
alternative. Of the three choices (start deck rules, arbitrary local
rules, and tournament rules), tournament rules are the least problematic.

Claiming that you have to remove zillions of good cards from the
game because they are "broken" when THE RULES THEMSELVES ARE BROKEN
is nonsensical to the extreme. I believe something like "penny
wise, pound foolish" comes to mind. Of course this sort of behavior
is not inconsistent if you are more concerned with tournament
play than that of the rest of the base, something I claim and you deny.

-*-
charles

