In article <3qdrf6$3...@darkstar.UCSC.EDU>,

Thomas R Wylie <aa...@cats.ucsc.edu> wrote:

} The deck construction rules for Magic are only necessary in any sense once
} you get into wild, trading environments where people are buying cards by
} the boxful, not a couple of boosters at the time. While this is unfortunate,
} it's not terribly surprising given that no one was expecting people to
} routinely spend hundreds of dollars buying Magic cards.

But given that this "wild, trading environment" is basically *everywhere*
doesn't it make sense to acknowledge it? You *know* people trade to get
the cards they want and that spending $70 (at least) on cards is no big
deal even for kids. Why pretend it isn't happening?

} But the best solution
} to the situation is not to saddle the game with deck construction rules.
} The best solution is to balance the card set to the point where deck
} construction rules are not necessary at all, and leave those rules for
} tournament environments where people are playing with the older, broken cards.

Tom, you continue to miss the point. Without deck construction rules
even non-broken cards lead to broken decks. It does *not* take buying
cards by the boxful to put together 20 Swamp, 20 Dark Ritual, 20 Drain Life
decks. It doesn't! I could put one together for a dollar. It's not
even particularly hard to acquire 5 or 10 Mind Twists.

-*-
