In article <3qg0rd$i...@darkstar.UCSC.EDU>,

Thomas R Wylie <aa...@cats.ucsc.edu> wrote:
}

} Charles M. Kozierok <i...@rice-chex.ai.mit.edu> wrote:

} >} The deck construction rules for Magic are only necessary in any sense once
} >} you get into wild, trading environments where people are buying cards by
} >} the boxful, not a couple of boosters at the time. While this is unfortunate,
} >} it's not terribly surprising given that no one was expecting people to
} >} routinely spend hundreds of dollars buying Magic cards.

} >But given that this "wild, trading environment" is basically *everywhere*
} >doesn't it make sense to acknowledge it? You *know* people trade to get
} >the cards they want and that spending $70 (at least) on cards is no big
} >deal even for kids. Why pretend it isn't happening?
}
} We're not denying that it's happening. The point is that it has become
} the norm, and the question becomes how best to account for what actually
} happens with trading card cards, namely the wild environment. Our opinion

} is that it is better to balance the card set than to burden the game
} with deck construction rules.

Two points:
1. "Balancing the card set" ignores the installed base of thousands of
players, who are not going to bonfire their cards and play with 4E.
Period. And it is this sort of comment that leads to accusations like
"WotC only cares about what happens in tournaments."
2. There is no way to balance the card set without taking out every card
that is good.

} >Tom, you continue to miss the point. Without deck construction rules
} >even non-broken cards lead to broken decks. It does *not* take buying
} >cards by the boxful to put together 20 Swamp, 20 Dark Ritual, 20 Drain Life
} >decks. It doesn't! I could put one together for a dollar. It's not
} >even particularly hard to acquire 5 or 10 Mind Twists.
}
} And my point is that while a Drain Life deck might be dull, it's probably
} not going to win a disproportionate amount of the time, so is not broken
} from a game balance point of view.

There are oodles of degenerate deck designs using even 4E that whack
other balanced decks a disproportionate amount of time. I think
10 Mishra's, 10 Plains, 10 Balances and 10 Racks might do pretty well.
There are oodles of other examples. Picking one example and
saying it doesn't seem that good just avoids the point.

} Deck construction rules should not
} be *necessary* for a game to be balanced; whether people tack them on to
} keep decks more interesting is another matter entirely.

Well, I think WotC should stop insisting that the construction
rules "*shouldn't* be necessary" while the rest of the world sees
so obviously that they *are*.

cheers,

-*-
charles

